INT. DEPARTMENT STORE ELEVATOR - DAY

An old brass-and-mahogany elevator with a folding gate. A mirror on every wall. Soft music from a hidden speaker.

MISS GWENDOLYN FAIRWEATHER, 30s, in a bright hat and white gloves, is the lift operator. HERBERT PUMPHREY, 50s, a floorwalker with a carnation in his lapel, stands in the corner clutching a hatbox.

GWENDOLYN
Third floor. Ladies' gloves, hosiery, and sundries.

HERBERT
I am not going to the third floor.

GWENDOLYN
Then why did you press three?

HERBERT
I did not press three. I pressed the nine.

GWENDOLYN
There is no nine.

HERBERT
There is a nine. The nine is the executive offices.

GWENDOLYN
The executive offices are the eighth.

HERBERT
Miss Fairweather, I have worked in this store for twenty-two years.

GWENDOLYN
And in twenty-two years you've never learned the numbers?

HERBERT
I know the numbers perfectly. It is you who has moved them.

GWENDOLYN
I operate the lift, Mr. Pumphrey. The floors are the floors.

HERBERT
Then explain the hatbox.

GWENDOLYN
What hatbox?

HERBERT
The one I am holding.

GWENDOLYN
You are holding that?

HERBERT
It was given to me by a woman on the second floor. She said, "Take this to the manager." She did not say which manager.

GWENDOLYN
Then it is likely for Mr. Trout.

HERBERT
Mr. Trout retired.

GWENDOLYN
Then it is for his son.

HERBERT
His son is on the ninth.

GWENDOLYN
Which does not exist.

HERBERT
You see my difficulty.

GWENDOLYN
I see two difficulties, Mr. Pumphrey, and neither is mine.

HERBERT
What are they?

GWENDOLYN
You are carrying a box you cannot deliver, and you have been riding up and down for an hour.

HERBERT
It has been forty minutes.

GWENDOLYN
It has been an hour. I have a log.

HERBERT
Then you are keeping a log of me?

GWENDOLYN
Of the lift. You happened to be in it.

HERBERT
Miss Fairweather, I am not accustomed to being a happenstance.

GWENDOLYN
Mr. Pumphrey, I am not accustomed to being accused of misplacing a floor.

HERBERT
Then what shall we do?

GWENDOLYN
Open the box.

HERBERT
It is not mine to open.

GWENDOLYN
Whose is it?

HERBERT
The manager's.

GWENDOLYN
Which manager?

HERBERT
That is the very question.

GWENDOLYN
Then it belongs to whoever opens it first.

HERBERT
That is not the law.

GWENDOLYN
It is the law of the elevator.

HERBERT
Since when?

GWENDOLYN
Since now.

HERBERT
(after a pause)
Hold the box.

GWENDOLYN
I'm operating the lift.

HERBERT
Hold it with one hand.

GWENDOLYN
I only have two, Mr. Pumphrey, and both are in use.

HERBERT
Then I shall open it myself.

He lifts the lid. The music swells, a harp glissando, unaccountably. Gwendolyn leans in. They both look.

GWENDOLYN
Well?

HERBERT
It is a hat.

GWENDOLYN
What sort of hat?

HERBERT
A very ugly hat.

GWENDOLYN
Whose?

HERBERT
(reading the label)
Yours.

Gwendolyn takes off her bright hat, looks at it, looks at the hatbox, and begins to laugh. After a moment Herbert, appalled, joins her.

The elevator stops. The gate rattles. Neither moves.
